<div class="exercise"><div class="ex-q"><p>Why not simply add many integrators to track everything?</p>
</div><details><summary>Show the solution</summary><div class="ex-s"><p>Each integrator adds 90° of phase lag at all frequencies. Two pure integrators already sit at −180°, so a type 2 loop needs zeros (phase lead) to be stable at all, as the <span class="katex"><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="katex-base"><span class="katex-strut" style="height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;"></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">s</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right:0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">+</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right:0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="katex-base"><span class="katex-strut" style="height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;"></span><span class="mord">1</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span> in the worked example provides. Three or more get progressively harder to stabilise and respond with large overshoot.</p>
</div></details></div>
